Important architecture note: Your current SQLAlchemy Session is synchronous. Its database operations can still block the event loop. This example keeps the existing database implementation for learning purposes. Later, we'll introduce async SQLAlchemy or move synchronous database work into a threadpool.
5. Implement streaming LLM responses
Normally, the API waits until the entire AI response has been generated.
Streaming changes that.
Instead of:
</>Plain text
User submits prompt
       ↓
Wait for complete AI response
       ↓
Return complete response

We want:
</>Plain text
User submits prompt
       ↓
AI starts generating
       ↓
First text arrives
       ↓
More text arrives
       ↓
Final text arrives
This is useful for chat applications, coding assistants and document analysis tools.
 
Step 5.1 – Create services/streaming_service.py
</>Python
import json

from openai import OpenAIError

from config.llm_config import (
    async_client,
    OPENAI_MODEL
)


async def stream_ai_response(prompt: str):

    try:

        stream = await async_client.responses.create(
            model=OPENAI_MODEL,
            instructions=(
                "You are a professional enterprise AI assistant. "
                "Provide clear and useful responses."
            ),
            input=prompt,
            stream=True
        )

        async for event in stream:

            if event.type == "response.output_text.delta":

                yield (
                    "event: token\n"
                    f"data: {json.dumps({'text': event.delta})}\n\n"
                )

            elif event.type == "response.completed":

                usage = event.response.usage

                usage_data = {
                    "input_tokens": (
                        usage.input_tokens if usage else 0
                    ),
                    "output_tokens": (
                        usage.output_tokens if usage else 0
                    ),
                    "total_tokens": (
                        usage.total_tokens if usage else 0
                    )
                }

                yield (
                    "event: completed\n"
                    f"data: {json.dumps(usage_data)}\n\n"
                )

            elif event.type == "response.failed":

                yield (
                    "event: error\n"
                    f"data: {json.dumps({'message': 'AI generation failed'})}\n\n"
                )

    except OpenAIError as error:

        print(
            f"Streaming error: {type(error).__name__}"
        )

        yield (
            "event: error\n"
            f"data: {json.dumps({'message': 'AI provider request failed'})}\n\n"
        )

This uses Server-Sent Events (SSE). Each event has a type and a data payload.
The client can receive:
•	token – a piece of generated text.
•	completed – generation finished and usage is available.
•	error – an error occurred.
